== The Final Forty — Complete Solutions

#sol-item(1, "A")[
  *Chapter 1.* Yeast is a single-celled fungus, so it has a true nucleus — bacteria are prokaryotic and *Spirogyra* and mosses are multicellular.
]

#sol-item(2, "B")[
  *Chapter 2.* Every cell has a plasma membrane; the other three are eukaryotic features.
]

#sol-item(3, "D")[
  *Chapter 3.* Pairing (synapsis) happens in prophase I; this step does not exist in mitosis at all.
]

#sol-item(4, "C")[
  *Chapter 4.* A thin leaf gives a short diffusion path both to the mesophyll cells and out again, which keeps the rate of gas exchange high.
]

#sol-item(5, "B")[
  *Chapter 5.* Gastric juice is strongly acidic (pH 1.5–2), which suits pepsin and kills most bacteria.
]

#sol-item(6, "C")[
  *Chapter 6.* As water evaporates from cell surfaces, the remaining cell sap becomes more concentrated, so water is drawn towards it.
]

#sol-item(7, "A")[
  *Chapter 7.* Platelets trigger the conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin, which forms a mesh over the wound.
]

#sol-item(8, "D")[
  *Chapter 8.* Air travels through spiracles into tracheae and tracheoles that reach almost every cell — no blood pigment needed.
]

#sol-item(9, "B")[
  *Chapter 9.* ADH makes the collecting duct more permeable to water, so more water is reabsorbed and the urine becomes more concentrated.
]

#sol-item(10, "D")[
  *Chapter 10.* Exoskeletons protect and support but must be shed (moulted) during growth.
]

#sol-item(11, "A")[
  *Chapter 1.* *Euglena* has chlorophyll but no cellulose wall, and in the dark it absorbs dissolved food — so it cannot be a plant.
]

#sol-item(12, "C")[
  *Chapter 2.* The pancreas is an organ made of several tissues that secretes digestive enzymes and hormones.
]

#sol-item(13, "D")[
  *Chapter 3.* Meiosis II is essentially a mitotic division of haploid cells; reduction happened in meiosis I.
]

#sol-item(14, "B")[
  *Chapter 4.* 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, with light energy trapped by chlorophyll.
]

#sol-item(15, "C")[
  *Chapter 5.* Circular muscle contracts behind the bolus and longitudinal muscle shortens in front, pushing the food forward.
]

#sol-item(16, "A")[
  *Chapter 6.* Water always flows from higher (less negative) to lower water potential; pure water is the reference point, at zero.
]

#sol-item(17, "A")[
  *Chapter 7.* Valves snap shut when the pressure falls, preventing backflow.
]

#sol-item(18, "D")[
  *Chapter 8.* Complete oxidation to carbon dioxide and water releases about 18 times as much usable energy per glucose.
]

#sol-item(19, "C")[
  *Chapter 9.* The kidneys are the main organs of osmoregulation, adjusting urine volume and concentration.
]

#sol-item(20, "B")[
  *Chapter 10.* Turgor pressure is the plant's hydraulic skeleton; when water content falls, support falls with it.
]

#sol-item(21, "C")[
  *Chapter 1.* Saprophytes digest *outside* the body and absorb the products; this is why moulds soften bread and fruit.
]

#sol-item(22, "B")[
  *Chapter 2.* More membrane means more embedded enzymes and electron carriers, so more ATP per unit volume.
]

#sol-item(23, "A")[
  *Chapter 3.* Crossing over shuffles alleles within a chromosome; independent assortment shuffles whole chromosomes between gametes.
]

#sol-item(24, "D")[
  *Chapter 4.* Sucrose made in the leaf travels in the phloem to roots, fruits and growing points.
]

#sol-item(25, "C")[
  *Chapter 5.* Bile salts are emulsifiers, not enzymes: they divide large fat globules so that lipase has far more surface to act on.
]

#sol-item(26, "D")[
  *Chapter 6.* Heat raises the rate of evaporation, dry air steepens the diffusion gradient and wind carries the moist layer away.
]

#sol-item(27, "A")[
  *Chapter 7.* A tough double membrane with a fluid layer reduces friction as the heart beats.
]

#sol-item(28, "B")[
  *Chapter 8.* Counter-current flow keeps the oxygen concentration of the water above that of the blood along the whole length of the gill lamella.
]

#sol-item(29, "A")[
  *Chapter 9.* Sweat glands are coiled glands in the dermis whose ducts open at pores on the skin surface.
]

#sol-item(30, "C")[
  *Chapter 10.* Costal cartilage joins most ribs to the sternum, allowing the rib cage to move in breathing.
]

#sol-item(31, "B")[
  *Chapter 1.* Latinised names are stable and used worldwide, so *Zea mays* means the same thing in Abuja, Delhi and São Paulo.
]

#sol-item(32, "D")[
  *Chapter 2.* A ribosome is two subunits of RNA and protein; it is never enclosed by a membrane.
]

#sol-item(33, "D")[
  *Chapter 3.* Halving keeps the number constant across generations after fertilisation; crossing over and assortment supply variation.
]

#sol-item(34, "C")[
  *Chapter 4.* On a dull day light is limiting, but extra carbon dioxide raises the ceiling and increases the rate until light becomes limiting again.
]

#sol-item(35, "B")[
  *Chapter 5.* Incisors are chisel-shaped with a single root — the cutting teeth at the front of the mouth.
]

#sol-item(36, "A")[
  *Chapter 6.* Evaporation creates a tension at the top; the cohesive water column is pulled up as a whole.
]

#sol-item(37, "B")[
  *Chapter 7.* A single layer of endothelium gives a short diffusion path, and their slow flow gives time for exchange.
]

#sol-item(38, "D")[
  *Chapter 8.* Fermentation by yeast gives ethanol and carbon dioxide; the gas bubbles are trapped in the gluten and the dough rises.
]

#sol-item(39, "A")[
  *Chapter 9.* Filling stimulates detrusor muscle to contract and the sphincter to relax, under voluntary control in adults.
]

#sol-item(40, "C")[
  *Chapter 10.* Most plant support comes from lignified xylem; sclerenchyma fibres add strength to older stems.
]

